/-!
# Arithmetic Correspondence Positivity

Umbrella for the positivity theory of arithmetic correspondences: normalized graphs and the free
theorem of the square; budgets and their sufficiency; determinant, Rosati and spectral degrees and
the complex fake; the local geometry of reflection pairs (boosts, rotations, resonance, isometries);
the unipotent successor; genus zero of affine maps; non-localizing and source-blind positivity;
`ζ` as the worst twin on nonnegative tests; the loss law of tensor powers; resolution and budget
horizons along orbits; sparse annihilators; the cofactor Gram as a translation Gram with its
Laplace symbol; the family loss law; escape states of dominant configurations, their rank, their
horizon averages and the coupling of dilations with the archimedean flow on them; power rows of
the zeros as dominant configurations; completeness of infinite summable configurations; the escape
completeness criterion; the escape alternative; section counts and Stepanov counts as the sign; the successor defect in the strip; the gauge-charged window off the edge; elliptic localization as resonance; Abel escape states as
boundary spectral measures of the symbol, with the dominant zeros of `ζ` as their atoms and the
escape rate as their capacity; Abel cofactor Grams and the Abel completeness criterion; the
endpoint law of escape; residue-mass laws for the Mertens and prime-error energies and their
`𝔽_q` calibration (the prime-error budget counts the spectrum); the successor defect as a bounded
Hardy perturbation; the boundary receiver's energy rate priced by the critical values at the
shadows; the Mertens budget law (a linear Mertens energy forces purity and a simple spectrum); the
prime-error budget dominates the archimedean genus `2 + γ − log 4π`; the genus-one Mertens
budget is the strict Hasse bound, and in every genus the Mertens slope is the repulsion energy of
the spectrum and a Mertens budget is purity with a simple spectrum; the Abel–Cramér law (the Abel slope of the
prime error is the archimedean genus exactly under RH with simple zeros). See
`ArithmeticCorrespondencePositivity/README.md`.
-/
